She sank victorious—her torn pennons yet aflame in the west, her guns black-lipp’d and reeking, her decks strewn thick with splinter’d spars, red cordage, shatter’d steel, and men whose warm blood ran in trembling threads to mingle with the cold, unconquer’d sea;  

Around her roll’d the wreckage of triumph—drums burst open, muskets bent like reeds, prayer-books sodden, bright buttons, sever’d hands still clutching rope, and the pale upturn’d faces of the young, on whose salt-stung lashes Death had hung his bitter dew;  

Her captain stood ’neath the riven mast, one arm bound close, his coat grown black with gore, and heard from far across the smoking waste the victors’ cheer—thin, ghostly, already less than the groan of timbers, the hiss of drowning fires, the deep bell tolling where no hand remain’d to pull;  

Then lower bow’d the mighty hull, that iron-bosom’d queen, while through her wounds the green flood rush’d—through wardroom, magazine, chapel, and berth, over maps of proud dominion, over letters ne’er to be read at home, over bread, bone, gold braid, and the open mouths of the slain;  

O’er all there hung the sulphurous evening—copper sun, bruisèd cloud, gulls wheeling white as funeral linen—and the sea, which owneth neither laurel nor lament, lifted her once, twice, as a mother might lift a dying child, then drew her downward stern by stern;  

And still her banner show’d above the foam, torn through yet unbow’d, till mast and flag and last dim cry went under—victory upon her name, ruin within her heart, and naught behind but oil, ash, blood, and a widening ring where the immortal glory of men had vanish’d.
